# PCB manufacturing

This board is built with **standard through-hole vias** (open barrels).
Via-in-pad (filled, plated-over, VIPPO) is a different process and is
**not allowed**.

## Vias vs pads (default)

- Never place a via **on** or **in** an ordinary SMD pad: capacitor,
  resistor, or IC signal/power pin.
- Place the via **as close as possible** to the pad, then neck a short
  copper connection (dog-bone) from the pad to the via.
- The via **drill** must sit outside the pad. Merged copper from pad to
  via annular ring is fine; a hole in the paste area is not.
- When advising those parts, say "next to the pad", never "on the pad".

Open vias in a paste pad wick solder down the barrel (tombstones, voids,
starved joints). Filling and capping vias is a fab option we are not
buying.

## Exception — footprints that require pad vias

When the datasheet or footprint **needs** vias in an exposed thermal pad,
put them there. Do not dog-bone those around the pad.

Applies today to **U3** (TPS54560-Q1 PowerPAD / pin 9): multiple vias
directly under the package, per datasheet §10.1 (thermal table assumes
6 × 13 mil). Tent or plug the barrels so paste is not sucked through;
we are still not buying filled/plated-over via-in-pad.

Same exception for any later part whose land pattern calls for thermal
vias in the exposed pad. Ordinary two-terminal and IC-pin pads stay
under the default ban.

## Defaults (KiCad netclass)

Board vias in use are ~0.7 mm / 0.3 mm drill. Default class is 0.6 mm /
0.3 mm drill, 0.2 mm clearance. Do not invent tighter geometry or
microvias unless the fab stack-up is updated.
